.\" seteuid.2 - set the effective user or group ID
.TH SETEUID 2 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
seteuid, setegid \- set the effective user or group ID
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int seteuid(uid_t " euid ");"
.br
.BI "int setegid(gid_t " egid ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR seteuid ()
sets the effective user ID of the calling process to
.IR euid ,
and
.BR setegid ()
its effective group ID to
.IR egid .
The real and saved IDs are not changed, which is what lets a set-user-ID
program give up its privilege for a while and take it back.
.PP
A process whose effective user ID is 0 may set either ID to any value.
Any other process may set the effective user ID only to its real,
effective or saved user ID, and the effective group ID only to its real,
effective or saved group ID.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.  On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The process is not privileged and the ID is not one of its real,
effective or saved IDs.
.SH EXAMPLE
A set-user-ID-root program does part of its work as the user who ran it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(void)
{
    if (seteuid(getuid()) == \-1) {     /* drop */
        perror("seteuid");
        return 1;
    }
    /* ... work as the invoking user ... */
    if (seteuid(0) == \-1) {            /* take it back */
        perror("seteuid");
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setuid (2),
.BR setreuid (2),
.BR setresuid (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2001, POSIX.1-2008, 4.3BSD.
